---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/usgaap/asc912.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Allowable vs Unallowable Costs (FAR Part 31 Cost Principles)" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Cost Accounting Standards (CAS) — Consistency and Disclosure" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Contract Types — Cost-Reimbursement, Fixed-Price, Time-and-Materials" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Claims and Requests for Equitable Adjustment (REAs)" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Cost Allocation — Direct Costs and Indirect Cost (Overhead) Pools" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Worked Example — Allowable vs Unallowable Cost Classification" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Worked Example — Claim Recognition" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s12", label: "No Direct IFRS Equivalent — Discussion" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Related Topics" },
        { id: "s15", label: "Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Accounting shaped by a specific federal regulatory regime</div><div>Contractors performing work for the US FEDERAL GOVERNMENT operate under a distinctive regulatory overlay — the FEDERAL ACQUISITION REGULATION (FAR) and, for larger contractors, the COST ACCOUNTING STANDARDS (CAS) — that governs which COSTS may be BILLED to (and reimbursed by) the government. ASC 912 addresses how this REGULATORY cost-allowability framework interacts with GENERAL GAAP cost recognition, particularly for cost-reimbursement-type contracts where the CONTRACT PRICE itself is substantially determined by allowable costs incurred.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Contractors performing under federal government contracts</div><div>ASC 912 applies to entities performing contracts directly with (or as subcontractors under contracts with) agencies of the US FEDERAL GOVERNMENT, subject to FAR and, where applicable, CAS.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Allowable vs Unallowable Costs (FAR Part 31 Cost Principles)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A regulatory allowability overlay on top of GAAP cost recognition</div><div>FAR Part 31 designates certain categories of cost as EXPRESSLY UNALLOWABLE for government contract billing purposes — even though those SAME costs may be entirely legitimate, ordinary business expenses recognised NORMALLY under GAAP. Common UNALLOWABLE cost categories include certain ENTERTAINMENT costs, ALCOHOLIC BEVERAGES, certain LOBBYING and public relations costs, BAD DEBTS, and certain EXECUTIVE COMPENSATION amounts exceeding specified caps. A cost being GAAP-recognisable does NOT mean it is RECOVERABLE from the government under a cost-reimbursement contract — these are genuinely separate questions, and ASC 912 requires contractors to track and account for this distinction.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Cost Accounting Standards (CAS) — Consistency and Disclosure</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A separate regulatory cost-measurement regime for larger contractors</div><div>Larger federal contractors are subject to the COST ACCOUNTING STANDARDS (CAS) — a set of 19 specific standards governing how costs must be MEASURED, ASSIGNED, and ALLOCATED to government contracts, requiring CONSISTENCY in cost accounting practices across contracts, and mandating a formal CAS DISCLOSURE STATEMENT describing the contractor's cost accounting practices. CAS compliance is a genuinely SEPARATE regulatory regime layered alongside — not a substitute for — general GAAP cost recognition.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Contract Types — Cost-Reimbursement, Fixed-Price, Time-and-Materials</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Different risk allocation, different revenue recognition considerations</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Contract type</span><span>Description</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Cost-reimbursement</span><span>The government reimburses the contractor's ALLOWABLE costs, plus a fee — the contractor bears less cost-overrun risk, but is exposed to potential cost DISALLOWANCE</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Fixed-price</span><span>The contractor is paid a FIXED amount regardless of actual costs incurred — the contractor bears the FULL risk of cost overruns, similar to ordinary ASC 606 fixed-price contract accounting</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Time-and-materials</span><span>The government pays specified HOURLY rates for labor plus the cost of materials — a hybrid risk allocation</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Claims and Requests for Equitable Adjustment (REAs)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Recognised only where recovery is probable and reasonably estimable</div><div>Federal contractors frequently pursue CLAIMS or REQUESTS FOR EQUITABLE ADJUSTMENT (REAs) — seeking additional compensation from the government for events such as GOVERNMENT-CAUSED delays, changes in scope, or differing site conditions. Consistent with the general contract accounting principles in ASC 910/606, additional consideration from a claim/REA is recognised only when recovery is PROBABLE and the amount is REASONABLY ESTIMABLE — a genuinely CONSERVATIVE recognition threshold given the inherent uncertainty of government contract dispute resolution processes.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Cost Allocation — Direct Costs and Indirect Cost (Overhead) Pools</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Consistent, auditable allocation bases required</div><div>Costs DIRECTLY traceable to a specific contract (direct labor, direct materials) are charged DIRECTLY to that contract. Costs benefiting MULTIPLE contracts (facilities costs, general management) are accumulated in INDIRECT COST (overhead) POOLS and ALLOCATED to individual contracts using a consistent, auditable ALLOCATION BASE (e.g. direct labor hours or dollars) — subject to potential government AUDIT of the contractor's cost accounting practices and allocation methodology.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example — Allowable vs Unallowable Cost Classification</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A GAAP expense that isn't billable to the government</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A contractor's client entertainment expenses</div><div class="wk-body">A federal contractor incurs $50,000 of client entertainment expenses (dinners, event tickets) during the year, alongside $2,000,000 of ordinary direct labor and materials costs on a cost-reimbursement contract.</div><div class="wk-answer">Under GENERAL GAAP, the $50,000 entertainment cost is a legitimate business EXPENSE, recognised normally in the income statement. However, under FAR Part 31, entertainment costs are EXPRESSLY UNALLOWABLE — the contractor CANNOT bill this $50,000 to the government for reimbursement under the cost-reimbursement contract, even though it remains a properly recognised GAAP expense. The $2,000,000 of ordinary direct costs, by contrast, IS allowable and reimbursable.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example — Claim Recognition</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Applying the probable-and-estimable threshold</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A delay claim against the government</div><div class="wk-body">A contractor incurs $800,000 of additional costs due to a government-caused delay in providing required site access, and submits a formal claim for $800,000 in additional compensation. Based on precedent and legal counsel's assessment, recovery is deemed PROBABLE, and the amount is reasonably estimable at $650,000 (after anticipated negotiation).</div><div class="wk-answer">The contractor recognises additional contract consideration of <strong>$650,000</strong> — the amount deemed probable AND reasonably estimable — rather than the full $800,000 claimed, reflecting the conservative recognition threshold applied to government contract claims.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 912 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Description of significant accounting policies related to government contract cost allowability and allocation</span><span>Nature and status of significant claims or REAs pending against the government</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
    <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Classifying costs as allowable or unallowable under FAR Part 31</span><span>Assessing whether recovery of a claim or REA is probable</span></div></div>
    <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Amount reasonably estimable for a pending claim or REA</span><span>Allocation of indirect costs to individual contracts using an appropriate base</span></div></div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>No Direct IFRS Equivalent — Discussion</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A regulatory framework tied specifically to US federal procurement law</div><div>IFRS has NO dedicated standard paralleling ASC 912 — the FAR and CAS regulatory regimes are SPECIFIC to US FEDERAL government procurement law, without a directly analogous structure in most other jurisdictions' public procurement frameworks. An IFRS reporter with government contracts would apply GENERAL IFRS 15 revenue recognition principles, without a dedicated allowable-cost overlay comparable to FAR Part 31. This makes ASC 912 one of the narrower, genuinely US-specific Topics in this library with NO direct IFRS counterpart, similar to ASC 808 (Collaborative Arrangements) and ASC 852 (Reorganizations).</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 912 event lands</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Unallowable costs</span><span><span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Still recognised as GAAP expenses, simply not billable/reimbursable</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Claim/REA recognition</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Recognised only when probable and reasonably estimable</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 910</span><span>Contractors—Construction — supplies the broader over-time revenue recognition and loss provision framework this Topic builds on</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 606</span><span>Supplies the general variable consideration framework applied to claims and REAs</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Allowable cost</div><div class="glossary-def">A cost permitted to be billed to and reimbursed by the federal government under FAR Part 31 cost principles.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Cost Accounting Standards (CAS)</div><div class="glossary-def">A set of federal regulatory standards governing cost measurement, assignment, and allocation practices for larger government contractors.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Request for equitable adjustment (REA)</div><div class="glossary-def">A contractor's formal request for additional compensation from the government, typically for government-caused delays or scope changes.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.asc912 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
